from lib import *
C=9;S='science'
lesson(C,S,'motion','equations-of-motion',"Speed, velocity, acceleration and the equations of motion","The cyclist","A cyclist speeds up from rest to 6 m/s in 3 seconds. How quickly did the speed change, and how far did she go? The equations of motion answer both.",
["Distance is the total path length (scalar); displacement is the shortest straight-line distance with direction (vector). @ distance and displacement",
 "Speed = distance / time; velocity = displacement / time. @ speed and velocity",
 "Acceleration a = (v - u)/t, where u is initial and v final velocity; unit m/s squared. @ a = (v - u)/t",
 "The three equations of motion for uniform acceleration: v = u + at, s = ut + 1/2 at squared, v squared = u squared + 2as. @ v = u + at",
 "For a freely falling body, a = g = 9.8 m/s squared downwards. @ g = 9.8",
 "In a distance-time graph the slope is the speed; in a velocity-time graph the slope is the acceleration and the area under it is the distance. @ graphs",
 "Uniform circular motion has constant speed but changing velocity, so it is accelerated. @ circular motion"],
("A car starts from rest and accelerates at 2 m/s squared for 5 s. Find its final velocity and the distance covered.",[("u = 0, a = 2, t = 5. v = u + at = 0 + 2 x 5 = 10 m/s.","First equation of motion."),("s = ut + 1/2 at squared = 0 + 1/2 x 2 x 25.","Second equation."),("s = 25 m.","Check: average speed 5 m/s x 5 s = 25 m.")]),
("State the three equations of motion and when they apply.",["v = u + at","s = ut + 1/2 at^2","v^2 = u^2 + 2as"],["Using them when acceleration is not constant"]),
["Speed is scalar, velocity is vector.","a = (v - u)/t.","v = u + at; s = ut + 1/2 at^2; v^2 = u^2 + 2as.","Free fall: a = g = 9.8 m/s^2."],
["n|A body at rest accelerates at 3 m/s^2 for 4 s. Final velocity (m/s)|12|v = at.",
 "t|Displacement can be zero even when the distance travelled is not.|True|A round trip returns to the start.",
 "m|The slope of a distance-time graph gives|speed|acceleration~That is the velocity-time slope;mass~Not on the graph|Distance divided by time.",
 "n|Distance (m) covered in 2 s by a body starting at rest with a = 4 m/s^2|8|1/2 x 4 x 4.",
 "t|Uniform circular motion is not accelerated.|False|Direction changes, so velocity changes.",
 "n|Acceleration (m/s^2) when velocity changes from 10 to 20 m/s in 5 s|2|10/5.",
 "n|Speed (m/s) of a body covering 100 m in 20 s|5|100/20.",
 "s|The unit of acceleration is metre per second ____.|squared|m/s^2."])
lesson(C,S,'fluids','pressure-and-buoyancy',"Pressure, Pascal's law and buoyancy","The needle and the foot","A sharp needle pushes into cloth easily but a flat foot does not, even with the same force. Pressure depends on the area as well as the force.",
["Pressure = force / area; the unit is pascal (Pa), 1 Pa = 1 N/m squared. @ P = F/A",
 "Pressure in a liquid at depth h is P = h rho g, where rho is the density; it acts equally in all directions. @ P = h rho g",
 "Pascal's law: pressure applied to a confined fluid is transmitted equally in all directions; used in hydraulic lifts and brakes. @ Pascal's law",
 "Atmospheric pressure is about 101 325 Pa and is measured with a barometer. @ atmospheric pressure",
 "Buoyant force is the upward force on an object in a fluid; Archimedes principle says it equals the weight of the fluid displaced. @ Archimedes principle",
 "An object floats if its density is less than the liquid's, and sinks if its density is more. @ float and sink",
 "Density = mass / volume, and relative density = density of substance / density of water. @ density"],
("A force of 200 N acts on an area of 0.5 square metre. Find the pressure.",[("Pressure = force / area.","Use the formula."),("P = 200 / 0.5.","Substitute."),("P = 400 Pa.","The unit is pascal.")]),
("State Archimedes principle and Pascal's law.",["Buoyant force equals weight of fluid displaced","Pressure transmitted equally","P = F/A"],["Thinking pressure depends on the shape of the container"]),
["P = F/A in pascal.","Liquid pressure P = h rho g.","Archimedes: buoyant force = weight of displaced fluid.","Floats if density is less than the fluid's."],
["n|Pressure (Pa) for a 100 N force on 2 square metres|50|100/2.",
 "t|Liquid pressure increases with depth.|True|P = h rho g.",
 "m|The SI unit of pressure is|pascal|newton~Unit of force;joule~Unit of energy|N/m^2.",
 "t|An object floats if its density is greater than the liquid's.|False|It floats if its density is less.",
 "n|Density (kg/m^3) of 500 kg occupying 0.5 m^3|1000|500/0.5.",
 "t|Pascal's law is used in hydraulic brakes.|True|Pressure transmitted through the fluid.",
 "n|Pressure (Pa) at 2 m depth in water (rho = 1000, g = 10)|20000|2 x 1000 x 10.",
 "s|The principle stating buoyant force equals the weight of fluid displaced is ____ principle.|Archimedes'/Archimedes|Famous Greek scientist."])
lesson(C,S,'light','reflection-and-mirrors',"Reflection of light and spherical mirrors","The bathroom mirror","In a plane mirror you see yourself the same size, and left appears right. A curved spoon, though, shows you upside-down and smaller. Mirrors follow clear laws.",
["Laws of reflection: the angle of incidence equals the angle of reflection, and the incident ray, reflected ray and normal lie in the same plane. @ laws of reflection",
 "A plane mirror forms a virtual, erect image the same size as the object, as far behind the mirror as the object is in front. @ plane mirror",
 "A concave mirror curves inwards and converges light; a convex mirror curves outwards and diverges light. @ concave and convex",
 "The focal length f of a spherical mirror is half its radius of curvature R, f = R/2. @ f = R/2",
 "Mirror formula: 1/v + 1/u = 1/f, and magnification m = - v/u. @ 1/v + 1/u = 1/f",
 "A concave mirror is used in shaving mirrors, torches and headlights; a convex mirror in vehicles as a rear-view mirror because it gives a wider field of view. @ uses",
 "Rough surfaces scatter light in many directions (diffuse reflection). @ diffuse reflection"],
("A concave mirror has a radius of curvature 20 cm. Find its focal length.",[("f = R / 2.","Relation for spherical mirrors."),("f = 20 / 2.","Substitute R."),("f = 10 cm.","The principal focus lies 10 cm from the mirror.")]),
("State the two laws of reflection and name the mirror used in vehicles.",["Angle i = angle r","Rays and normal in one plane","Convex mirror gives wide view"],["Measuring angles from the mirror instead of the normal"]),
["Angle of incidence = angle of reflection.","Plane mirror: virtual, erect, same size.","f = R/2.","Convex mirrors give a wider view."],
["n|Focal length (cm) of a mirror with radius of curvature 30 cm|15|R/2.",
 "t|The image in a plane mirror is virtual.|True|It cannot be caught on a screen.",
 "m|Vehicles use which mirror as a rear-view mirror?|convex|concave~It gives a narrow view;plane~Gives a smaller field|Wide field of view.",
 "n|Angle of incidence is 35 degrees. Angle of reflection (degrees)|35|Equal angles.",
 "t|The angle of incidence is measured from the mirror surface.|False|It is measured from the normal.",
 "t|Concave mirrors converge parallel rays to the focus.|True|That is why they are used in torches.",
 "n|If R = 40 cm, f (cm) =|20|R/2.",
 "s|A mirror that curves inwards is a ____ mirror.|concave|Definition."])
lesson(C,S,'heat','heat-temperature-and-specific-heat',"Heat, temperature, specific heat and change of state","The kettle","A kettle on a flame warms the water until it boils. Heat is energy that flows because of a temperature difference.",
["Temperature measures how hot a body is; heat is the energy transferred between bodies because of a temperature difference. @ heat and temperature",
 "Temperature is measured in degrees Celsius (C) or kelvin (K): K = C + 273. @ K = C + 273",
 "Heat moves by conduction (in solids), convection (in liquids and gases) and radiation (no medium needed). @ conduction, convection, radiation",
 "Specific heat capacity c is the heat needed to raise 1 kg of a substance by 1 K; the heat required is Q = m c delta T. @ Q = m c delta T",
 "Water has a high specific heat, about 4200 J/kg K, so it heats and cools slowly. @ water 4200",
 "Latent heat is absorbed or released during change of state at constant temperature; melting, boiling and freezing are examples. @ latent heat",
 "Most substances expand on heating; water is unusual between 0 and 4 degrees Celsius. @ expansion"],
("How much heat is needed to raise 2 kg of water by 10 degrees Celsius? (c = 4200 J/kg K)",[("Q = m c delta T.","Heat formula."),("Q = 2 x 4200 x 10.","Substitute."),("Q = 84 000 J.","About 84 kJ.")]),
("Explain the three modes of heat transfer with an example each.",["Conduction in solids","Convection in fluids","Radiation needs no medium"],["Saying heat and temperature are the same"]),
["Heat is energy; temperature is hotness.","K = C + 273.","Q = m c delta T.","Heat transfer: conduction, convection, radiation."],
["n|Convert 27 degrees Celsius to kelvin|300|27 + 273.",
 "t|Radiation needs a medium to travel.|False|The Sun's heat reaches us through empty space.",
 "n|Heat (J) to warm 1 kg of water by 2 K (c = 4200)|8400|1 x 4200 x 2.",
 "m|Heat flow in a liquid by moving currents is|convection|conduction~Through solids;radiation~By waves|Convection currents.",
 "t|Temperature and heat are the same thing.|False|Heat is energy and temperature is a measure of hotness.",
 "n|0 degrees Celsius in kelvin is|273|0 + 273.",
 "t|Water has a high specific heat capacity.|True|It is about 4200 J/kg K.",
 "s|Heat transfer in solids happens by ____.|conduction|Particle vibrations."])
lesson(C,S,'sound','sound-waves-and-echo',"Sound waves, frequency, speed and echo","The temple bell","A temple bell rings and the sound travels outwards in all directions. Sound needs a medium and travels as waves of compressions and rarefactions.",
["Sound is produced by vibrating bodies and needs a material medium - it cannot travel in a vacuum. @ medium needed",
 "Sound waves in air are longitudinal: particles vibrate along the direction of travel, forming compressions and rarefactions. @ longitudinal wave",
 "Frequency is the number of vibrations per second (hertz); the time period T = 1/f. @ frequency",
 "Speed of sound = frequency x wavelength (v = f x lambda). @ v = f lambda",
 "Sound travels fastest in solids, slower in liquids and slowest in gases; in air at room temperature it is about 343 m/s. @ speeds",
 "Loudness depends on amplitude; pitch depends on frequency; humans hear 20 Hz to 20 000 Hz. @ loudness and pitch",
 "An echo is the reflection of sound heard after at least 0.1 s, so the obstacle must be at least about 17 m away. @ echo"],
("A sound has a frequency of 500 Hz and a wavelength of 0.68 m. Find its speed.",[("v = f x wavelength.","Wave equation."),("v = 500 x 0.68.","Substitute."),("v = 340 m/s.","Matches the speed of sound in air.")]),
("Explain how sound travels and what an echo is.",["Compressions and rarefactions","v = f lambda","Echo needs 0.1 s delay"],["Thinking sound travels in a vacuum"]),
["Sound needs a medium and is longitudinal.","v = f x wavelength.","Loudness: amplitude; pitch: frequency.","Echo: reflected sound after at least 0.1 s."],
["n|Speed (m/s) of a wave with f = 200 Hz and wavelength 1.5 m|300|200 x 1.5.",
 "t|Sound can travel through a vacuum.|False|It needs a medium.",
 "m|Pitch depends on|frequency|amplitude~That decides loudness;speed~Not pitch|Higher frequency means higher pitch.",
 "n|Time period (s) of a sound of frequency 50 Hz as decimal|0.02|1/50.",
 "t|Sound travels faster in solids than in air.|True|Particles are closer.",
 "n|Frequency (Hz) of a wave with period 0.01 s|100|1/0.01.",
 "t|An echo is heard when the reflected sound arrives within 0.05 s.|False|It must arrive after at least 0.1 s.",
 "s|The number of vibrations per second is the ____.|frequency|Unit hertz."])
lesson(C,S,'atomic-structure','atomic-models-and-electron-arrangement',"Atomic models, subatomic particles and electron arrangement","The tiny solar system","Early scientists pictured the atom as a solid ball. Experiments showed instead a tiny dense nucleus with electrons around it, rather like planets around the Sun.",
["Atoms contain protons (positive charge, in the nucleus), neutrons (no charge, in the nucleus) and electrons (negative charge, moving around the nucleus). @ proton, neutron, electron",
 "Dalton thought of atoms as indivisible; Thomson proposed the plum-pudding model; Rutherford discovered the dense positive nucleus using alpha particles; Bohr proposed electrons in fixed orbits. @ models",
 "Atomic number Z = number of protons (= number of electrons in a neutral atom); mass number A = protons + neutrons. @ Z and A",
 "Electrons fill shells K, L, M, N with capacities 2, 8, 18 and 32 (2n squared). @ 2, 8, 18",
 "Sodium (Z = 11) has the arrangement 2, 8, 1, and chlorine (Z = 17) has 2, 8, 7. @ Na 2,8,1",
 "Isotopes are atoms of the same element with the same atomic number but different mass numbers; isobars have the same mass number but different atomic numbers. @ isotopes and isobars",
 "Valency is the combining capacity of an atom, determined by the outer-shell electrons. @ valency"],
("Write the electron arrangement of magnesium (Z = 12) and find the number of neutrons in Mg-24.",[("12 electrons fill K = 2, L = 8, then M = 2.","Fill shells in order."),("Arrangement is 2, 8, 2, so valency is 2.","Outer shell has 2 electrons."),("Neutrons = mass number - atomic number = 24 - 12 = 12.","A = p + n.")]),
("Describe Rutherford's model and what mass number and atomic number mean.",["Small dense positive nucleus","Z = protons","A = protons + neutrons"],["Counting electrons as part of the mass number"]),
["Protons + neutrons are in the nucleus.","Z = protons; A = protons + neutrons.","Shell capacities 2, 8, 18, 32.","Isotopes: same Z, different A."],
["n|Neutrons in an atom with Z = 6 and A = 14|8|14 - 6.",
 "t|Electrons have a positive charge.|False|They are negative.",
 "m|Rutherford discovered the|nucleus|electron~Thomson found it;neutron~Found later by Chadwick|Alpha-particle scattering.",
 "n|Maximum electrons in the M shell|18|2n^2 with n = 3.",
 "t|Isotopes have the same atomic number.|True|Same protons, different neutrons.",
 "n|Electrons in a neutral atom with atomic number 17|17|Equal to protons.",
 "n|Mass number of an atom with 6 protons and 6 neutrons|12|6 + 6.",
 "s|Atoms of the same element with different mass numbers are ____.|isotopes|Definition."])
lesson(C,S,'organisation-of-tissues','plant-and-animal-tissues',"Plant and animal tissues","The building blocks","A brick wall is made of bricks, and the body is made of cells. Groups of similar cells doing one job together make a tissue.",
["A tissue is a group of similar cells that together perform a particular function. @ tissue",
 "Plant tissues are meristematic (cells that keep dividing, at the tips of roots and shoots) and permanent (cells that have taken a fixed shape and job). @ meristematic and permanent",
 "Simple permanent tissues: parenchyma (storage), collenchyma (flexibility) and sclerenchyma (strength). @ simple tissues",
 "Complex permanent tissues: xylem carries water and minerals up; phloem carries food from the leaves to other parts. @ xylem and phloem",
 "Animal tissues are epithelial (covering and lining), connective (blood, bone, cartilage), muscular (movement) and nervous (messages). @ animal tissues",
 "Muscles may be striated (skeletal, voluntary), smooth (involuntary, in organs) or cardiac (heart, involuntary). @ muscle types",
 "Several tissues together form an organ and organs working together form an organ system. @ organ and system"],
("Which plant tissue carries water, and which carries food?",[("Water and minerals from the roots travel upwards in the xylem.","Xylem has hollow vessels."),("Food made in the leaves moves to the rest of the plant through the phloem.","Phloem carries sugars both up and down."),("So xylem carries water and phloem carries food.","Both are complex permanent tissues.")]),
("Name the main plant and animal tissues and their jobs.",["Xylem and phloem","Epithelial, connective, muscle, nerve","Meristem divides"],["Thinking the xylem carries food"]),
["Tissue = group of similar cells with one function.","Meristem divides; permanent tissues have fixed jobs.","Xylem: water; phloem: food.","Animal tissues: epithelial, connective, muscle, nervous."],
["m|The tissue that carries water in plants is|xylem|phloem~Carries food;collenchyma~Gives flexibility|Xylem.",
 "t|Meristematic tissue is found at the tips of roots and shoots.|True|It keeps dividing.",
 "m|Cardiac muscle is found in the|heart|stomach~Smooth muscle;arm~Skeletal muscle|Heart.",
 "t|Phloem transports food.|True|From leaves to other parts.",
 "m|Blood is a|connective tissue|epithelial tissue~Covers surfaces;muscular tissue~Not a muscle|Fluid connective tissue.",
 "t|Organs form from organ systems.|False|Tissues form organs, and organs form organ systems.",
 "t|Smooth muscle is involuntary.|True|We cannot control it.",
 "s|A group of similar cells with one function is a ____.|tissue|Definition."])
